  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 237 PM CDT Sun Aug 23 2026\n\nA pair of waves are affecting the area, or nearby, today. The first \nis moving southeast through Nebraska and is keeping any instability \nto the south and west bottled up. Model soundings not hinting at a \nwhole lot of activity this afternoon, with a fairly dry and stable \nair mass in place.\n\nTonight a second wave will pass by to the north and will swing a \nlittle bit of mid level moisture into the area. As this moisture \nmoves in, some weak elevated instability, likely 500-750 J/kg CAPE \ndevelops. This will support some scattered shower and isolated \nthunderstorm activity, but for now severe weather looks\nunlikely. A few pockets of heavier rain are also possible, but\nalso trending downward.\n\nMonday will see temperatures warm a bit with humidity likely to \nincrease as well. This is in response to strengthening southerly \nflow as a stronger wave moves east along the Canadian border. This \nwill increase instability, especially to the northwest of the area, \nand should bring scattered thunderstorms to parts of central SD and \nnear the highway 14 corridor. With the potential for about 1000-1500 \nJ/kg CAPE and decent unidirectional shear, and isolated severe storm \nwill be possible in these locations. One area to watch will be near \nnorthwest IA late Monday afternoon and evening where there are some \nhints the cap could weaken a bit as elevated moisture advection \nbring some instability to that area. Low confidence, but something \nto monitor.\n\nNorthwest flow aloft Tuesday into the weekend will keep seasonally \nwarm temperatures in place with highs in the 80s and lows from the \nupper 50s to mid 60s. Starting to see a little more consistency on \nthe next better chance for showers and thunderstorms on Friday. The \nlatest Canadian, GEFS and EC ensembles all indicate about a 30-50\npercent chance for a tenth of an inch or more of precipitation.",
